  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q29-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "29",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p21-q29-subtitle",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Supermarket principle",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ],
              "colour": "#8b5cc7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p21-q29-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A supermarket has unlimited apples, bananas and oranges. Mary buys exactly nine items in total.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p21-q29-divider-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "At the checkout, she lines up the nine items and places two identical dividers to split them into three groups (apples, bananas, oranges). Dividers may be placed at either end or next to each other, so any of the three groups may be empty.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-q29-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why the number of possible purchases is the same as the number of ways to choose the two divider positions in a sequence of eleven symbols: nine identical item symbols and two identical dividers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Each purchase corresponds to exactly one sequence of nine item symbols and two dividers. The three group sizes give the numbers of apples, bananas and oranges; adjacent or end dividers allow zero items of a type.",
          "worked": "Represent each item by an identical symbol and separate the apple, banana and orange groups with two dividers, in that order. A purchase determines exactly one such sequence. Conversely, a sequence determines the three quantities by counting symbols before, between and after the dividers. End or adjacent dividers allow an empty group. Therefore purchases and divider arrangements correspond one-to-one.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q29-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "How many ways can Mary buy nine items?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{11}{2}=55$ ways.",
          "worked": "The combined sequence contains nine item symbols and two dividers. Choose the two divider positions among its eleven positions:\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\binom{11}{2}\\\\N&=55.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
